      "@type": "Dataset",
      "name": "Medical & Laboratory Equipment Relocation Operations",
      "description": "Medical and laboratory equipment relocation operations — how the heavy physical layer of a medical or lab move is run at The Flat Rate Movers, from Winchester VA and Martinsburg WV. The published chain has seven steps: (1) survey and structural check — every unit inventoried with weight, dimensions and center of mass, the route measured at both ends, and floors on the load path checked against L/360, span divided by 360, before any lift is planned; heavy units of 1,500 to 3,500 lbs are planned under SIR (Structural Integrity Relocation) and the KIM-GS Protocol; (2) vendor and EHS coordination — the schedule is built around the manufacturer's or biomedical team's de-installation window and the facility's own protocols, and the facility's decontamination sign-off, where its EHS team requires one, is a gate in the sequence; that sign-off is issued by the facility, never by the mover; (3) de-installation support — the crew works alongside the vendor's technicians, positioning, supporting and rigging components as they are released, while the technical de-installation itself is performed by the vendor; (4) pack and custom crate — instruments and components packed under padded instrument covers and, where the item or route calls for it, custom-crated with foam-lined or foam-in-place protection, electronic instruments in anti-static, ESD-safe packing and sensitive units sealed in moisture-barrier wrap, with ISPM 15 export packing where a chain continues internationally; the packing materials a job consumes are purchased for each project and scoped inside the flat rate; (5) sealed, documented transport — loads sealed for the run, instrument crates carrying tilt and shock indicator labels read at each hand-off, condition documented at each hand-off point; (6) placement and leveling — each unit set in its final position and leveled; (7) vendor recommissioning — the manufacturer's technicians or the facility's biomedical engineers reconnect, calibrate, certify and recommission. Steps 2, 3 and 7 are coordination with the customer's specialists, not services performed by the mover: no calibration, certification, commissioning, decontamination or requalification is performed or claimed at any step. Every engagement is surveyed in full and quoted per job at one flat rate in writing before work begins, never by the hour. Not published and not claimed: transport of specimens, chemicals, reagents or hazardous or biohazardous material; transport of cryogenic contents of any kind; temperature-controlled or powered cold-chain transport; any equipment list — equipment varies by project; any handling rule for heavy units outside the published SIR band of 1,500 to 3,500 lbs.",
